  "how_each_value_was_chosen": {
    "structure": "Trees start from the normal-approximation baseline (init_score). Study: candidate `base` against `v1_no_platt` (log loss 0.44913 against 0.44973) and `scores_no_base_leaf2000` against `scores_leaf2000` (0.44944 against 0.44863).",
    "baseline_sigma_and_tau": "Fitted by maximum likelihood on the training rows of every fit (two numbers). Fixing tau instead was worse: 30 seconds without the rules (`base_sharp`, 0.44968), and 60, 120 or 240 seconds with them (`rules_tau*`: 0.44869 to 0.44929, larger end-game gaps, and the 13-second check further from passing).",
    "baseline_kneel_rule": "On. Where the leader with the ball can kneel out (kneel_slack >= 0), the baseline is certain. On development seasons those rows were won 3,313 times in 3,314 (study, final_checks and docs/notes/m1_wp.md). Candidate `rules_kneel_leaf2000` against `scores_leaf2000`: log loss equal (0.44863), end-game gap 0.0048 against 0.0051, kneel-out check passed (minimum 1.000 against 0.924).",
    "baseline_possession_value": "Off. A fitted value for having the ball (about 1.6 points) did not help once the trees were in place (`rules_both_leaf2000` 0.44880, `rules_poss_leaf2000` 0.44874).",
    "features": "is_home dropped, kneel_slack, possessions and possessions_per_minute added. See decisions below.",
    "min_child_samples": "2000. Not the overall log-loss winner of the tuning grid: 5000 scored 0.44852 against 0.44863 (a difference of 0.0001), but its last-2:00 log loss is clearly worse (0.2362 against 0.2338), and the end game is what version 2 is for. 1000 is slightly better in the last 2:00 (0.2331) with a larger memorization gap (study: 0.0061 against 0.0057). Leaf sweep in the study: 5000, 2000, 1000, 500, 200.",
    "num_leaves": "15. Tuning grid: 15 beats 31 at every leaf size (0.44863 against 0.44883 at 2000).",
    "n_estimators_and_learning_rate": "300 trees at 0.06, kept from version 1. Tuning grid at 2000 leaves: 150 trees at 0.12 scored 0.44872, 600 at 0.03 scored 0.44857, against 0.44863. The 600-tree gain (0.00006) is noise-sized and doubles the fit time that the M3 bootstrap pays 101 times, so 300 is kept by a stated reason, not as the winner.",
    "colsample_bytree_subsample_reg_lambda": "0.8, 0.8 (every round) and 10. Not tuned: common settings kept from version 1 so the number of looks at the data stays small. Stated, not chosen by a score.",
    "max_bin": "127. Not tuned; halves the fit time against LightGBM's 255.",
    "monotone_constraints_method": "intermediate. Not tuned; LightGBM's middle setting between the fast basic method and the slow advanced one.",
    "monotone_constraints": "Set by reasoning, not by a score: more points, a better spread and more timeouts for the team with the ball can never hurt it; more defensive timeouts never help.",
    "SPREAD_DECAY": "4.0, kept from version 1, where it was set by hand with no recorded comparison. It only shapes one of several spread features, and the trees can undo it. Not tuned.",
    "TIME_FLOOR_SECONDS": "30, kept. Study: a 5-second floor made no difference with the baseline in place (`base_floor5` 0.44933 against `base_no_home` 0.44930; `kneel_floor5_leaf2000` 0.44881 against `kneel_leaf2000` 0.44877).",
    "kneel_constants": "40-second play clock, 2 seconds per kneel, 120-second cap: rules and measurements in docs/research/endgame_clock.md, not fitted to outcomes.",
    "KNEEL_SLACK_FLOOR": "-600 seconds. Stated: more than ten minutes from a kneel-out is all the same for this feature, and capping it keeps the trees' bins on the end game.",
    "possessions_per_minute_offset": "30 seconds added to the clock, the same as TIME_FLOOR_SECONDS, so the feature stays finite at 0:00. Not tuned.",
    "BASELINE_LOGIT_CAP": "9 (about 1 in 8,000). Stated, keeps log-odds finite; not tuned.",
    "calibration": "none. See decisions.calibration.",
    "seed": "Fixed project seed."
  },
